# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday: a climb beyond your skill (calls UR, cycle s4)

== the last pitch as the weather turns
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult adult
age: 18-45
calls: UR
tone: mixed
life: leisure, body, nature
horizon: moment
roles: friend, partner, a mountain guide
earth: halfway up a ridge beyond your skill, the summit is an hour away and the cloud is coming in
times: Uncommon: perhaps 1 in 8 adults, mostly keen walkers and climbers, once or a few times (estimate)
gap: 6-12
share: .12
rate: .5
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: Halfway up the ridge the rock turns slick and the cloud is coming in fast. The summit is an hour away, and {friend} is already reaching for the next hold.
scene W: The guidebook says to turn back if the cloud comes down below the col, and it is below the col now. {N} wrote the turn-back time on the back of {Ns} hand this morning.
scene U: {N} studies the map: the ridge narrows past the next tower, and the way down from the top needs clear weather to find.
scene B: Three summers of planning went into this summit, and everyone in the club has done this ridge. Turning back means doing it all again next year.
scene R: The wind is roaring, the drop on either side is enormous, and {Ns} whole body is singing.
scene G: The mountain is closing in around them, grey and patient. It has been here a long time, and it will still be here next summer.
- W | keep to the plan the whole party agreed at breakfast: the summit, then down | v: conformity | mark: took a wild risk | body: heavy | death_if_fails: 0.01 | chance: 70
- U | check the map and the forecast again, and pick the safer gully down | v: security | chance: 76
- B | push on for the summit; three summers of planning are not to be wasted | v: achievement | mark: took a wild risk | body: heavy | death_if_fails: 0.01 | chance: 68
- R | climb on into the cloud, whooping, and trust your hands | v: stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | body: heavy | death_if_fails: 0.01 | chance: 66
- G | climb on slowly, feeling for each hold, as the mountain allows | v: universalism (nature), security | mark: took a wild risk | body: heavy | death_if_fails: 0.01 | chance: 66
- W>G | rope everyone together, and lead the slowest down first | v: benevolence, security | body: heavy | chance: 76
- U>W | radio your position to the rescue team, then go on with care | v: security | body: heavy | death_if_fails: 0.005 | chance: 70
- B>U | pay the guided party ahead for a place on their rope | v: security, achievement | self_control: + | chance: 78
- R>B | race your friend down the scree to settle who is faster | v: power, stimulation | body: heavy | chance: 80
- G>R | drop off the ridge to the tarn below, and swim in it before the rain | v: hedonism, universalism (nature) | body: light | chance: 80
works: Everyone is off the mountain by dusk, soaked, laughing and starving.
works: The cloud lifts for a minute at the top, and {N} sees half the country spread out below.
fails: Someone slips on the wet rock, and the day ends with a helicopter and a stretcher.
fails: The cloud comes down thick, and they spend a long cold night lost on the hill.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday: the car keys after drinks (calls WR, cycle s1)

== the car keys after a few drinks
tier: everyday
at: police
stages: young_adult adult
age: 18-50
calls: WR
holds: driving licence
tone: trouble
life: leisure, body, law
horizon: moment
roles: friend, parent, the landlord, the police
earth: the pub is closing, the car is outside, you have had a few, and home is twelve miles of country road away
times: Many drivers who drink, now and then over the years; about 1 in 50 US adults drove after drinking too much in the past month (CDC 2011)
gap: 3-6
scene: The pub is closing. The car is in the car park, home is twelve miles of dark country road away, and {N} has had more than one.
scene W: The law has a limit, and {N} has always kept to it. Tonight {N} honestly cannot say which side of it they are on.
scene U: {N} counts back over the evening: when each drink was, how big, how long ago. The sum comes out differently every time.
scene B: A taxi out here costs a fortune and takes an hour, and the car would have to be fetched in the morning anyway.
scene R: {N} feels fine, the night is warm, and the road home is one {N} could drive with their eyes shut.
scene G: {friend} is slurring a little and wants a lift. Outside, the lane is black between the hedges, and an owl is calling.
- W | leave the car, phone for a taxi, and wait however long it takes | v: conformity, security | self_control: + | chance: 82
- U | blow into the pub's breathalyser machine, and drive if it says yes | habit | v: security | self_control: - | mark: took a wild risk | body: heavy | death_if_fails: 0.002 | suspends_if_fails: driving licence | for_years: 1 | closed: law: driving over the limit; backfire: the machine was wrong, and the police are not | law: drink-driving | chance: 76
- B | sleep it off on the back seat, and drive home at first light | v: security | self_control: + | chance: 76
- R | talk the landlord into a lock-in, and worry about the car tomorrow | v: hedonism | chance: 72
- G | ask the landlord, an old friend, for the sofa in the back room | v: security, tradition | chance: 74
- W>U | wait on water for as long as the rule of thumb says, then drive | v: security | suspends_if_fails: driving licence | for_years: 1 | chance: 70
- U>B | drive, with mints and a story ready in case the police stop the car | v: security, power | self_control: - | mark: broke the law | body: heavy | death_if_fails: 0.002 | suspends_if_fails: driving licence | for_years: 1 | title_if_fails: someone with a record | closed: law: drink-driving; backfire: the story does not hold up | law: drink-driving | chance: 76
- B>R | take the back lanes where nobody checks, and be home in twenty minutes | habit | v: hedonism, self-direction | mark: broke the law | body: heavy | death_if_fails: 0.004 | suspends_if_fails: driving licence | for_years: 1 | title_if_fails: someone with a record | closed: law: drink-driving; backfire: a crash, or a ban and a record | law: drink-driving | chance: 76
- R>G | walk your friend home along the lanes, singing, however long it takes | v: benevolence, hedonism | body: light | self_control: + | chance: 80
- G>W | ring home for a lift, however late, the way the family always said to | v: security, tradition | chance: 78
works: {N} wakes up at home the next morning, and the night ends there.
works: Everyone gets home safe, and by lunchtime the car is back where it belongs.
fails: A police car pulls out behind {N} on the long straight by the reservoir, lights flashing.
fails: No lift comes, the night turns cold, and it is nearly dawn before {N} gets home.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a friend who will not wake (pairs D2a)

== a friend who will not wake up
tier: life event
stages: juvenile young_adult adult
age: 15-35
per_year: 0 .004 .008 .002 0 0
drivers: ties+.2 era+.2 unrest+.2 community-.2 trouble+.1
gap: 3-10
times: estimate: about 4 in 1,000 teenagers, 8 in 1,000 young adults and 2 in 1,000 adults a year are the one who finds a friend they cannot wake after drink or drugs; the young carry most emergency visits for alcohol poisoning and overdose, and most of those brought in survive (NIAAA, CDC); about one or two in a hundred lives meet it more than once
likelier: friends who drink hard or use drugs, house parties and festivals, a scene where nobody calls for help
rarer: a sober circle, friends who look out for each other, a first-aid course
tone: trouble
life: friends, body, health
horizon: moment
roles: friend, parent, a paramedic, the police
earth: at four in the morning after a night out, a friend on the sofa will not wake up however hard you shake them
scene: It is four in the morning. {friend} is on the sofa, grey-faced and breathing strangely, and does not wake however hard {N} shakes them.
scene W: There is a number for exactly this. {N} knows the rule: call first, ask questions later.
scene U: {N} tries to think in order: breathing, colour, what was taken, how long ago. The facts are what will matter to whoever comes.
scene B: There are drugs on the table and {Ns} name is on the lease. An ambulance might bring the police with it.
scene R: {Ns} heart is hammering. {N} cannot just stand here; something has to be done, right now.
scene G: {N} kneels by {friend} and holds a cold hand. They have known each other since they were small.
- W | ring the emergency number at once, and do exactly what the operator says | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 62
- U | turn your friend on their side, watch the breathing, and call only if it worsens | v: security, benevolence | chance: 62
- B | hide the drugs and clear the flat before ringing anyone | v: security, power | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: hiding evidence, and a delay; backfire: the delay costs minutes that matter | chance: 64
- R | haul your friend into a cold shower to shock them awake | v: benevolence | body: heavy | chance: 60
- G | sit holding their hand, talking to them, until they stir | v: benevolence | closed: approval: not calling for help; backfire: they get worse, and everyone asks why nobody rang for help | chance: 66
- WU | phone for an ambulance, and tell them exactly what was taken and when | v: benevolence, security | mark: owned up | chance: 74
- WG | wake the neighbours, and get every grown-up in the building to help | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 62
- UR | flag down a taxi and get your friend to the hospital faster than any ambulance | v: benevolence | body: heavy | chance: 64
- BR | run, before anyone can say you were there | v: security, self-direction | mark: refused someone in need | closed: approval: leaving a friend in danger; backfire: someone saw, and everyone knows | chance: 76
- BG | ring your friend's family, and keep the doctors and the police out of it | v: security, tradition | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: keeping a friend in danger from the doctors; backfire: help comes late | chance: 60
works: {friend} comes round in the end, grey and shaking, and alive.
works: By the next afternoon {friend} is sitting up in a hospital bed, asking for toast.
fails: {friend} spends three days in intensive care, and {N} spends them in the corridor.
fails: The police come with the ambulance, and the questions go on until dawn.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: painkillers after an injury (pairs D2b)

# gate: an injury or an operation in the last year (a failed act with body: heavy, 'a serious accident or illness', 'an operation gives something back', 'an operation that makes things better', 'a fall at home')
== the last week of the painkillers
tier: life event
at: hospital
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-90
per_year: 0 0 .004 .008 .01 .008
drivers: health-.4 stress+.3 money-.2 era+.3 trouble+.15
gap: 5-15
times: estimate: about 4 in 1,000 young adults, 8 in 1,000 adults and 1 in 100 people from midlife on a year reach the end of a course of strong painkillers after an injury or an operation and still want them; about 6 in 100 people given opioids after surgery are still taking them three to six months later (Brummett 2017, JAMA Surgery), and prescribing has fallen since 2012 (CDC)
likelier: a bad back, a sports injury or an operation, a doctor generous with prescriptions, stress, an era of easy pills
rarer: a doctor who tapers early, physiotherapy and patience, a family that notices
tone: trouble
life: body, health
horizon: months
roles: a doctor, partner, friend, colleague, grandparent
earth: weeks after a bad injury the pain is mostly gone, the prescription is nearly finished, and you still want the pills
scene: The injury has mostly healed, and the bottle of painkillers is nearly empty. {N} counts what is left and feels a small, cold fear of the day it runs out.
scene W: The label says how many and how often, and {N} has kept to it, mostly. The doctor said the course ends this week.
scene U: {N} has started watching the clock: the hour before the next pill is the worst. {N} knows what that pattern means.
scene B: {N} knows which doctor at the practice signs repeat prescriptions without asking questions.
scene R: The pills take the edge off everything, not just the back. The week feels flat and grey without them.
scene G: The body heals in its own time. {grandparent} used to say that pain is a messenger, not an enemy.
- W | finish the course as written, and hand the leftovers back at the pharmacy | v: conformity, security | self_control: + | chance: 66
- U | keep a diary of the cravings, and come off them on a plan you draw up | habit | v: self-direction, security | self_control: + | chance: 60
- B | order more from an online pharmacy that asks no questions | habit | v: hedonism, security | mark: used drugs | body: heavy | death_if_fails: 0.002 | closed: law: prescription drugs without a prescription; backfire: the pills are fakes | self_control: - | law: drugs | tech: internet | chance: 66
- R | buy more from a friend of a friend when the bottle runs out | habit | v: hedonism | mark: used drugs | body: heavy | death_if_fails: 0.003 | closed: law: pills bought on the street; backfire: the pills are not what they seem | self_control: - | law: drugs | chance: 70
- G | let the pain come back, and go out walking with it every morning | habit | v: security, universalism (nature) | body: light | self_control: + | chance: 66
- RG | bin the last pills, and go up into the hills to sweat it out | v: self-direction, universalism (nature) | body: heavy | self_control: + | chance: 52
- UB | tell the other doctor a well-rehearsed story, for a new prescription | v: hedonism, security | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: lying to a doctor; backfire: the records are shared, and every prescription stops | self_control: - | chance: 66
- UG | ask the physiotherapist about other ways to live with the pain | door | v: security, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 70
- WB | sign up for the clinic's programme quietly, so nobody at work finds out | binds | v: security, achievement | self_control: + | chance: 58
- WR | tell your partner or closest friend everything, and hand them the bottle | binds | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: owned up | chance: 66
works: The last pill goes, the bad weeks pass, and {N} sleeps through a night again.
works: {N} comes off them slowly, and one morning notices that the clock has stopped mattering.
fails: The bottle runs out on a Friday, and the weekend is the longest of {Ns} life.
fails: By the spring the pills are part of every day, and {N} has stopped counting.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a heart scare and the drink (triads of D4a)

# gate: drinks heavily (a drinking act flagged habit chosen often in recent years, the pull of 'a craving that keeps calling'); never a lifelong non-drinker
== a racing heart after a heavy weekend
tier: life event
at: hospital
stages: adult mature elder
age: 35-90
per_year: 0 0 0 .002 .006 .008
drivers: stress+.4 health-.4 money-.1 trouble+.15
gap: 3-10
times: estimate: about 2 in 1,000 adults, 6 in 1,000 people in midlife and 8 in 1,000 elders a year end up in hospital with a racing or stumbling heart after heavy drinking (the "holiday heart" of Ettinger 1978); atrial fibrillation rises steeply with age, and each daily drink raises the risk by about 8 in 100 (Kodama 2011, Journal of the American College of Cardiology)
likelier: heavy weekends, high blood pressure, stress, a family history, age
rarer: light drinking, fitness, a doctor seen regularly
tone: trouble
life: body, health
horizon: months
roles: a doctor, partner, friend, parent, child
earth: on the Monday after a heavy weekend your heart starts racing and stumbling, and the hospital asks how much you drink
scene: On the Monday after a long weekend of drinking, {Ns} heart starts racing and stumbling like a bird against a window. By noon {N} is on a trolley with wires on their chest, and a young doctor is asking how much {N} drinks.
scene W: The doctor wants an honest number. {N} has always filled in forms truthfully, and the true number looks like a shock once it is written down.
scene U: {N} watches the ragged trace on the monitor and wants to know exactly what is going on in there, and what the drink has to do with it.
scene B: {N} knows the right number to give: low enough to sound sensible, high enough to be believed.
scene R: {N} is frightened, and furious at being frightened. Life without the weekends would hardly be life.
scene G: {parent} went the same way: a flushed face and a bad heart at sixty. {N} watches the screen and thinks of that.
- W | give the doctor the true number, and sign up for the clinic | habit | v: conformity, security | mark: owned up | chance: 64
- U | read everything on the heart and drink, and set your own strict limits | habit | v: self-direction, security | mark: learned a skill | self_control: + | chance: 64
- B | give the doctor a lower number, and carry on as before at home | habit | v: hedonism, security | mark: hid a wrong | self_control: - | chance: 62
- R | discharge yourself, go to the pub, and drink to being alive | v: hedonism, stimulation | mark: defied an authority | closed: approval: leaving against medical advice; backfire: the heart goes again, worse | self_control: - | chance: 68
- G | stop drinking quietly, and spend the weekends out of doors instead | v: security, universalism (nature) | self_control: + | chance: 65
- WUR | take on a dry year as a challenge, with a race at the end | binds | v: achievement, stimulation | self_control: + | chance: 50
- WUB | follow the cardiologist's plan to the letter, with the best specialist money can buy | binds | v: security, achievement | self_control: + | chance: 70
- WBG | tell the family, and agree a house rule: no drink in the house | v: security, tradition | self_control: + | chance: 60
- URG | learn to cook properly, cycle to work, and let the body mend | habit | v: self-direction, security | body: light | chance: 62
- BRG | decide that life is short, and keep the weekends exactly as they are | habit | v: hedonism, tradition (acceptance) | mark: took a wild risk | death_if_fails: 0.003 | self_control: - | chance: 55
works: The heart settles into its old rhythm, and {N} goes home with a leaflet and a new resolve.
works: Six months on, the trace on the screen is steady, and the doctor says so.
fails: Two weeks later the heart stumbles again, and this time the ambulance comes to the house.
fails: The weekends go on as before, and the pill boxes on the kitchen shelf multiply.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: driving with failing eyes (triads of D4b)

# gate: still drives: has('driving licence') (holds:) and has('car') or a household car
== the cyclist at dusk
tier: life event
stages: mature elder
age: 60-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 .005 .02
drivers: health-.5 era+.1
gap: 3-10
holds: driving licence
times: estimate: about 5 in 1,000 drivers in their sixties and 2 in 100 elders who still drive a year have a near miss they come to blame on their eyes; drivers with cataracts have about two and a half times the crash rate (Owsley 1999), and cataract surgery cuts it by about half (Owsley 2002, JAMA)
likelier: cataracts or macular disease, driving at dusk and in rain, a home far from the buses, no one else to drive
rarer: regular eye tests, a partner who drives, a town with good buses
tone: trouble
life: body, freedom
horizon: months
roles: child, partner, friend, a doctor, a stranger
earth: driving home at dusk, a cyclist appears at your bonnet out of nowhere, and you brake just in time
scene: At dusk on the ring road a cyclist appears at {Ns} bonnet as if out of nowhere. {N} stamps on the brake, the bike wobbles past, and the rider's shout carries through the closed window.
scene W: Every few years the licence form asks whether {Ns} eyes meet the standard, and {N} has always ticked yes.
scene U: {N} replays it: the cyclist had lights. They were there all along, and {Ns} eyes simply did not catch them.
scene B: Without the car {N} would depend on others for every errand, every visit, every scrap of independence.
scene R: {Ns} hands are shaking on the wheel, and {N} wants to roar at the cyclist, at the dark, at getting old.
scene G: The light goes earlier every autumn, and lately {Ns} eyes seem to go with it.
- W | report the near miss and your eyesight to the licensing office yourself | v: conformity, universalism | mark: owned up | takes: driving licence | chance: 70
- U | book an eye test for the next morning, and find out exactly what is wrong | v: self-direction, security | chance: 82
- B | say nothing, and stop driving after dark, so nobody need ever know | v: self-direction, security | mark: hid a wrong | chance: 76
- R | shout after the cyclist, then drive on home as if nothing happened | v: self-direction | chance: 74
- G | park the car, walk the last mile home, and leave the keys on the hook | v: security, tradition (acceptance) | body: light | chance: 74
- WUG | have the cataract operation the optician has been urging for years | v: security | chance: 75
- WRG | tell the family that night, and let them take the car keys | v: benevolence, security | chance: 80
- WBR | sell the car the same week, and spend the money on taxis | v: self-direction, security | self_control: + | chance: 85
- UBR | buy a car with every sensor and camera going, and keep on driving | v: self-direction, power | chance: 60
- UBG | map who in the street drives where, and trade favours for lifts | binds | v: security, benevolence | mark: made a friend | chance: 65
works: The fright passes, and {N} finds a way of getting about that frightens nobody.
works: Within a month {N} has settled into a new routine, and sleeps better for it.
fails: A month later {N} clips a parked van in the rain, and the questions start.
fails: {N} sits in the kitchen turning the car keys over, and the house feels very small.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: up the ladder (cross cycle s2)

# gate: ~has('living in residential care'), and a house with gutters to clear (has('homeowner') or a rented house)
== up the ladder to clear the gutters
tier: life event
stages: mature elder
age: 55-100
per_year: 0 0 0 0 .03 .05
drivers: health-.3 money-.2 ties-.2
gap: 2-5
times: estimate: about 3 in 100 people in midlife and 5 in 100 elders a year face a job up a ladder they are no longer steady enough for; well over 100,000 people a year in the United States are treated in hospital after a fall from a ladder, and the old are the likeliest to be admitted or to die of it (D'Souza 2007, American Journal of Preventive Medicine)
likelier: a house of one's own, living alone, little money for help, a lifetime of doing it oneself
rarer: a flat, family or neighbours close by, money for a firm, a bad fall already
tone: mixed
life: home, body
horizon: moment
roles: child, neighbour, partner
earth: autumn leaves have blocked the gutters again, the ladder is in the shed, and you are not as steady as you were
scene: The gutters are full of leaves again and water is spilling down the front wall. The old ladder is in the shed, and {N} is not as steady on their feet as they were.
scene W: {child} made {N} promise, after the last fall, never to go up a ladder alone again. The water keeps dripping all the same.
scene U: {N} knows the angle a ladder should stand at and how to tie it off. Knowing it and doing it at this age are two different things.
scene B: The firm that does gutters wants a day's wages for twenty minutes' work, and {N} did this job for forty years without paying anyone.
scene R: It is a bright, cold morning, {N} feels good today, and the job would take ten minutes.
scene G: Every autumn of {Ns} life the leaves have come down and someone has gone up to clear them. That someone was always {N}.
- W | keep your promise, and ring a firm to do it properly | v: conformity, security | mark: kept your word | chance: 88
- U | set the ladder at the right angle, tie it off, and go up slowly | v: self-direction, achievement | mark: took a wild risk | body: heavy | death_if_fails: 0.005 | chance: 82
- B | find someone at the pub who will do it for cash, no questions asked | v: security, power | chance: 74
- R | nip up the ladder right now and get it done before anyone can fuss | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | body: heavy | death_if_fails: 0.01 | chance: 72
- G | go up as you have every autumn of your life, and trust your hands | habit | v: tradition | mark: took a wild risk | body: heavy | death_if_fails: 0.01 | chance: 80
- W>B | ask the council's handyman scheme for older people, which costs next to nothing | v: security, achievement | chance: 60
- U>R | rig a pole and a hose to clear the gutter from the ground | v: self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 68
- B>G | guilt your grown child into coming over on Sunday to do it | v: security | chance: 76
- R>W | bang on the neighbour's door and talk them into doing it today, for lunch | v: benevolence, security | chance: 74
- G>U | watch where the water runs in the next storm, and learn what the house needs | v: self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 70
works: The gutters run clear by lunchtime, and the dripping stops.
works: The job gets done, and {N} sits down with a cup of tea feeling about forty.
fails: The ladder shifts on the wet path, and {N} ends up on the grass with a wrist that will not bend.
